//! crypto/hybrid_sealed.rye — Season G (Cryptography), the HYBRID sealed-to-identity door: a message sealed TO a hybrid identity so its confidentiality
//! survives a break in EITHER family — the classical X25519 key agreement Comlink uses today, AND the post-quantum ML-KEM agreement a lattice identity
//! carries. It is the confidentiality sibling of crypto/hybrid_identity.rye: where that makes a signature only genuine when BOTH the Ed25519 and the
//! ML-DSA halves verify (a forger must break both families), this seals a message only readable when BOTH the classical and the post-quantum agreements
//! are opened (an eavesdropper must break both families). The safe-by-default confidential door for the transition years the dual-key discovery named:
//! during the years between "the curves are fine" and "a quantum computer broke the curves," the strongest sealed box stands on both agreements, so a
//! break in either one — a surprise lattice attack, or the quantum day for the elliptic curves — still leaves the message sealed on the other
//! (active-designing/20260816-192400 — "hybrid, not either-or, is the safe default … a break in either family still leaves the identity standing on the other").
//!
//! It authors NO new cryptography and adds NO new primitive. Confidentiality is an ONION of two doors already GREEN: the inner layer is
//! crypto/kumara_sealed.rye (the CLASSICAL sealed box, addressed through the identity's Ed25519→X25519 half), and the outer layer is
//! crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye (the POST-QUANTUM sealed box, addressed through the identity's ML-KEM half). seal wraps the plaintext in the classical box,
//! then wraps THAT whole frame in the post-quantum box; open peels the post-quantum layer, then the classical one. To read the plaintext an adversary
//! must break BOTH: the outer ML-KEM box to reach the inner frame, AND the inner X25519 box to reach the plaintext. Break one family alone and the
//! message stays sealed — exactly the "both must fall" guarantee crypto/hybrid_identity.rye gives a signature, now given a sealed message.
//!
//! Season: the Six-Season double-seat, Season G thread (Cryptography · Rye-native, audit-ready). Double-seats BESIDE crypto/kumara_sealed.rye (the
//! classical confidential door) and crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye (the post-quantum one), retiring neither — a keeper who trusts one family alone uses that
//! door directly; the hybrid door is for the keeper who wants both agreements guarding one message at once.
//! Design read: active-designing/20260816-195850_the-hybrid-sealed-door-both-agreements.md (this door's own note) · active-designing/20260816-192400_post-quantum-identity-is-dual-key.md — the dual-key decision this door is the confidentiality construction of.
//! Kin: crypto/hybrid_identity.rye (the SIGNING sibling — a signature genuine only when both families verify, GREEN) · crypto/kumara_sealed.rye (the
//! classical inner layer, GREEN) · crypto/mlkem_sealed.rye (the post-quantum outer layer, GREEN) · crypto/kumara_pq_sealed.rye (the signed post-quantum
//! door). Clean-room: FIPS 203, RFC 7748/8032 are public standards studied through their specs; the onion is our own Rye wiring over our own GREEN rungs
//! (.claude/rules/gratitude-licenses.md).
//!
//! THE ONION — outer post-quantum ML-KEM box ⟨ inner classical X25519 box ⟨ plaintext ⟩ ⟩.
//!   seal: inner = kumara_sealed.seal_to(recipient Ed25519 public, fresh ephemeral) over the plaintext, then out = mlkem_sealed.seal(recipient ML-KEM ek,
//!   fresh KEM message) over that inner frame. The returned frame is a single Grain Post-Quantum sealed frame (magic "GPQ1") whose plaintext IS the
//!   classical sealed frame. Both the ephemeral X25519 private key and the ML-KEM message are FRESH randomness the caller supplies per message — this
//!   library invents no entropy. The bound is honest: a hybrid plaintext fits when the inner classical frame (plaintext + 60 bytes of classical overhead)
//!   fits the post-quantum box's own plaintext bound, so max_plain_bytes is the ML-KEM plaintext bound minus that classical overhead.
//!
//! HONEST SCOPE — software only, purely LOCAL, no network, no funds, no real device. Every identity in the selftest is a TEST hybrid identity from a
//! published-vector mnemonic; the module's own functions call no std.crypto (each layer is proven one rung down). Constant-time timing-safety stays
//! crypto/CONSTANT_TIME.md's named horizon; sealing to or opening as the maintainer's OWN hybrid identity, grown from a real phrase, stays the custody gate.
//!
//! Parity is proven at selftest over every parameter set with no fabricated constant: (1) a confidential ROUND-TRIP across plaintext lengths recovers the
//! message byte-for-byte through both layers; (2) CONFIDENTIALITY — the sealed frame body is not the plaintext; (3) BREAK-ONE-IS-NOT-ENOUGH — a holder of
//! ONLY the classical seed (no ML-KEM secret) fails at the outer post-quantum layer, and a holder of ONLY the ML-KEM secret (a wrong classical seed) peels
//! the outer layer yet fails at the inner classical one — an eavesdropper who breaks one family alone reads nothing; (4) TAMPER — a flipped frame byte
//! refuses; (5) DETERMINISM given fixed ephemerals, and a stranger identity refused.
//!
//!   env RYE_ZIG=vendor/zig-toolchain/zig rye/bin/rye run crypto/hybrid_sealed.rye selftest

const std = @import("std");
const assert = std.debug.assert;
const print = std.debug.print;

const hybrid = @import("hybrid_identity.rye");
const pq = @import("kumara_pq_identity.rye");
const classical = @import("kumara_identity.rye");
const inner_box = @import("kumara_sealed.rye"); // the classical inner layer
const outer_box = @import("mlkem_sealed.rye"); // the post-quantum outer layer

pub const hybrid_sealed_version = "20260816.195850";

/// The classical inner frame's fixed overhead (magic ‖ ephemeral pubkey ‖ ciphertext-length ‖ tag) — 60 bytes, inherited so the bound below is honest.
pub const inner_overhead: u32 = inner_box.frame_overhead;

/// The largest inner classical frame the outer post-quantum box can carry as its plaintext — the outer box's own plaintext bound. Named so seal sizes one
/// scratch buffer to this ceiling and no larger.
pub const max_inner_frame_bytes: u32 = outer_box.max_plain_bytes;

/// The largest hybrid plaintext one sealed carry holds: the outer box's plaintext bound minus the inner classical overhead, so the inner frame always
/// fits the outer box. This door adds no looser limit — it is strictly bounded by the tighter of the two layers.
pub const max_plain_bytes: u32 = outer_box.max_plain_bytes - inner_overhead;

/// The 32-byte KEM message width for the outer layer, borrowed so a caller sizes one value.
pub const seed_bytes: u32 = outer_box.seed_bytes;

/// The 32-byte ephemeral X25519 private-key width for the inner layer.
pub const ephemeral_bytes: u32 = 32;

/// max_frame_bytes — the whole hybrid envelope at the largest plaintext, per set: the outer post-quantum frame around a full inner frame. Since the inner
/// frame's maximum equals the outer box's plaintext bound, the hybrid maximum equals the outer box's own maximum frame. Named so a caller's scratch cites
/// one ceiling.
pub fn max_frame_bytes(comptime p: pq.PqParams) u32 {
    return outer_box.max_frame_bytes(p.kem);
}

pub const HybridSealedError = error{
    /// A plaintext longer than max_plain_bytes — refused before any key agreement, so the inner frame can never overrun the outer box.
    PayloadTooLong,
    /// seal's output buffer is too small to hold the whole outer frame — refused before any byte the caller relies on.
    OutTooSmall,
    /// open was handed a frame the outer post-quantum box could not even parse (too short, malformed length, or wrong magic).
    BadFrame,
    /// The OUTER post-quantum layer did not open — a wrong ML-KEM recipient, or a tampered outer frame. Reached by a holder of only the classical secret:
    /// breaking one family alone leaves the outer box sealed. No plaintext is revealed.
    OuterOpenFailed,
    /// The outer layer opened, yet the INNER classical layer did not — a wrong X25519 recipient, or a tampered inner frame. Reached by a holder of only
    /// the ML-KEM secret: peeling the post-quantum layer is not enough, the classical box still holds. No plaintext is revealed.
    InnerOpenFailed,
    /// A composed seal or open rung surfaced an error outside the expected refusals — honored honestly rather than swallowed.
    ComposedFailed,
};

/// What open() returns once BOTH layers have opened: a zero-copy slice of the recovered plaintext, pointing into the caller's own out buffer.
pub const Opened = struct {
    plain: []const u8,
};

/// seal_to — seal `plain` TO a hybrid identity named by its classical Ed25519 public key and its post-quantum ML-KEM encapsulation key, wrapping it in
/// the classical box first and the post-quantum box around that. Both `ephemeral_priv` (the inner X25519 ephemeral) and `m` (the outer KEM message) are
/// FRESH randomness the caller supplies per message. Returns the whole hybrid frame's length. A sender needs only the recipient's two PUBLIC keys.
pub fn seal_to(
    comptime p: pq.PqParams,
    recipient_ed_pub: *const [classical.public_len]u8,
    recipient_kem_ek: *const [pq.agree_public_len(p)]u8,
    ephemeral_priv: *const [ephemeral_bytes]u8,
    m: *const [seed_bytes]u8,
    plain: []const u8,
    out: []u8,
) HybridSealedError!u32 {
    // invariant (precondition): the plaintext fits the hybrid bound, so the inner frame (plaintext + inner_overhead) fits the outer box's plaintext bound.
    if (plain.len > max_plain_bytes) return error.PayloadTooLong;

    // INNER LAYER — seal the plaintext in the classical X25519 box into a bounded scratch frame.
    var inner: [max_inner_frame_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    const inner_len = inner_box.seal_to(recipient_ed_pub, ephemeral_priv, plain, inner[0..]) catch return error.ComposedFailed;
    // invariant (bound): the inner frame is no larger than the outer box's plaintext bound, so the outer seal below accepts it.
    assert(inner_len <= max_inner_frame_bytes);

    // OUTER LAYER — seal the whole inner frame in the post-quantum ML-KEM box into the caller's out buffer.
    const outer_len = outer_box.seal(p.kem, recipient_kem_ek, m, inner[0..inner_len], out) catch |e| return switch (e) {
        error.OutTooSmall => error.OutTooSmall,
        else => error.ComposedFailed,
    };
    // invariant (postcondition): the whole hybrid frame fits the caller's buffer.
    assert(outer_len <= out.len);
    return outer_len;
}

/// open_as — recover the plaintext of a hybrid sealed frame, peeling the outer post-quantum layer with the recipient's ML-KEM secret, then the inner
/// classical layer with the recipient's Ed25519 seed. BOTH must open: a holder of only the ML-KEM secret peels the outer layer yet fails the inner
/// (InnerOpenFailed); a holder of only the classical seed fails the outer layer first (OuterOpenFailed). Returns a zero-copy slice of the plaintext.
pub fn open_as(
    comptime p: pq.PqParams,
    recipient_ed_seed: *const [classical.secret_len]u8,
    recipient_kem_dk: *const [pq.agree_secret_len(p)]u8,
    recipient_kem_ek: *const [pq.agree_public_len(p)]u8,
    frame: []const u8,
    out: []u8,
) HybridSealedError!Opened {
    // OUTER LAYER — peel the post-quantum box into a bounded scratch, recovering the inner classical frame.
    var inner: [max_inner_frame_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    const outer_opened = outer_box.open(p.kem, recipient_kem_dk, recipient_kem_ek, frame, inner[0..]) catch |e| return switch (e) {
        error.FrameTooShort, error.LengthMismatch, error.BadMagic => error.BadFrame,
        error.OpenFailed => error.OuterOpenFailed,
        else => error.ComposedFailed,
    };

    // INNER LAYER — peel the classical box, recovering the plaintext into the caller's out buffer.
    const inner_opened = inner_box.open_as(recipient_ed_seed, outer_opened.plain, out) catch |e| return switch (e) {
        error.BadMagic, error.OpenFailed => error.InnerOpenFailed,
        else => error.ComposedFailed,
    };
    // invariant (postcondition): the recovered plaintext honours the hybrid bound (each layer enforced its own; restated at this door).
    assert(inner_opened.plain.len <= max_plain_bytes);
    return Opened{ .plain = inner_opened.plain };
}

/// seal_to_identity — the convenience door: seal TO a whole HybridType, reading its two published public keys. A sender holding a peer's hybrid identity
/// reaches seal_to without unpacking the fields.
pub fn seal_to_identity(
    comptime p: pq.PqParams,
    id: *const hybrid.HybridType(p),
    ephemeral_priv: *const [ephemeral_bytes]u8,
    m: *const [seed_bytes]u8,
    plain: []const u8,
    out: []u8,
) HybridSealedError!u32 {
    // invariant (construction): the identity's classical public key is the fixed Ed25519 width the inner door expects.
    assert(id.classical.public_key.len == classical.public_len);
    return seal_to(p, &id.classical.public_key, &id.post_quantum.agree.ek, ephemeral_priv, m, plain, out);
}

/// open_as_identity — the convenience door: open AS a whole HybridType, reading its classical seed and its ML-KEM secret. The holder of a hybrid identity
/// opens without unpacking the secrets by hand.
pub fn open_as_identity(
    comptime p: pq.PqParams,
    id: *const hybrid.HybridType(p),
    frame: []const u8,
    out: []u8,
) HybridSealedError!Opened {
    // invariant (construction): the identity's classical seed is the fixed width the inner secret map expects.
    assert(id.classical.secret_seed.len == classical.secret_len);
    return open_as(p, &id.classical.secret_seed, &id.post_quantum.agree.dk, &id.post_quantum.agree.ek, frame, out);
}

// ---- shared helpers -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

fn bytes_eq(a: []const u8, b: []const u8) bool {
    if (a.len != b.len) return false;
    var i: u32 = 0;
    // invariant (bound): read exactly a.len bytes, one per turn.
    while (i < a.len) : (i += 1) if (a[i] != b[i]) return false;
    return true;
}

/// Fill a buffer with a deterministic, non-trivial TEST pattern seeded per call so distinct payloads differ. TEST data only, never real randomness.
fn fill_test(buf: []u8, seed: u8) void {
    var i: u32 = 0;
    while (i < buf.len) : (i += 1) buf[i] = @as(u8, @truncate(@as(u32, i) *% 131 +% @as(u32, seed) +% 17));
    if (buf.len > 0) buf[0] |= 1;
}

// ---- selftest ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// BIP-39's own published Trezor vector 1 — a TEST phrase, never a real one.
const trezor_mnemonic = "abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon abandon about";

/// check_param — prove the hybrid sealed door for one parameter set: a confidential round-trip, the ciphertext not the plaintext, and the load-bearing
/// guarantee that breaking ONE family alone reveals nothing, plus tamper, determinism, and a stranger refused.
fn check_param(comptime p: pq.PqParams) !void {
    const test_coin: u32 = 7;
    // The recipient hybrid identity, and a DIFFERENT identity (a different passphrase), each a TEST hybrid identity. NOT real identities.
    const keeper = try hybrid.from_mnemonic(p, trezor_mnemonic, "RECIPIENT", test_coin, 0, 0);
    const stranger = try hybrid.from_mnemonic(p, trezor_mnemonic, "STRANGER", test_coin, 0, 0);

    // Fresh TEST randomness — in real use, fresh per message.
    var esk: [ephemeral_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(esk[0..], 3);
    var m: [seed_bytes]u8 = undefined;
    fill_test(m[0..], 19);

    var frame_buf: [max_frame_bytes(p)]u8 = undefined;
    var out_buf: [max_plain_bytes]u8 = undefined;

    // 1. CONFIDENTIAL ROUND-TRIP across a spread of plaintext lengths, including the empty message: sealed to the keeper through both layers, opened as
    //    the keeper through both, the plaintext returns byte-for-byte.
    const lengths = [_]u32{ 0, 1, 16, 64, 500, 4096 };
    for (lengths) |plen| {
        var plain_buf: [4096]u8 = undefined;
        const plain = plain_buf[0..plen];
        fill_test(plain, @truncate(plen +% 5));
        const flen = try seal_to_identity(p, &keeper, &esk, &m, plain, frame_buf[0..]);
        const opened = try open_as_identity(p, &keeper, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.plain, plain)) return error.PlainWrong;
    }

    // 2. CONFIDENTIALITY — the outer frame body is NOT the plaintext (the message is enciphered through both layers, not carried in clear).
    {
        var plain_buf: [128]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 9);
        const flen = try seal_to_identity(p, &keeper, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        const body_start = outer_box.header_len(p.kem);
        if (flen < body_start + 128) return error.FrameTooShortForBody;
        if (bytes_eq(frame_buf[body_start .. body_start + 128], plain_buf[0..])) return error.CiphertextIsPlaintext;
    }

    // 3. BREAK-ONE-IS-NOT-ENOUGH — the heart of the hybrid guarantee.
    {
        var plain_buf: [64]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 31);
        const flen = try seal_to_identity(p, &keeper, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);

        // 3a. An eavesdropper holding ONLY the classical seed (the keeper's real Ed25519 seed) yet the WRONG ML-KEM secret (the stranger's) cannot even
        //     peel the outer post-quantum layer — breaking the classical family alone leaves the outer box sealed: OuterOpenFailed.
        if (open_as(p, &keeper.classical.secret_seed, &stranger.post_quantum.agree.dk, &stranger.post_quantum.agree.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.ClassicalOnlyOpened;
        } else |e| if (e != error.OuterOpenFailed) return error.ClassicalOnlyWrongError;

        // 3b. An eavesdropper holding ONLY the ML-KEM secret (the keeper's real ML-KEM keypair) yet the WRONG classical seed (the stranger's) peels the
        //     outer layer, then fails the inner classical box — breaking the post-quantum family alone is not enough: InnerOpenFailed.
        if (open_as(p, &stranger.classical.secret_seed, &keeper.post_quantum.agree.dk, &keeper.post_quantum.agree.ek, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.PqOnlyOpened;
        } else |e| if (e != error.InnerOpenFailed) return error.PqOnlyWrongError;

        // The true keeper, holding BOTH halves, still opens it.
        const opened = try open_as_identity(p, &keeper, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..]);
        if (!bytes_eq(opened.plain, plain_buf[0..])) return error.TrueKeeperPlainWrong;
    }

    // 4. TAMPER — a flipped byte in the outer frame body breaks the post-quantum AEAD tag: OuterOpenFailed, no plaintext revealed.
    {
        var plain_buf: [48]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 41);
        const flen = try seal_to_identity(p, &keeper, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        frame_buf[outer_box.header_len(p.kem)] ^= 0x01;
        if (open_as_identity(p, &keeper, frame_buf[0..flen], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.TamperOpened;
        } else |e| if (e != error.OuterOpenFailed) return error.TamperWrongError;
    }

    // 5. STRANGER REFUSED and DETERMINISM. A frame sealed to the keeper is refused wholesale under the stranger's identity; and sealing is a pure
    //    function of its inputs — the same identity, ephemerals, and message seal to byte-identical frames.
    {
        var plain_buf: [80]u8 = undefined;
        fill_test(plain_buf[0..], 53);
        const f1 = try seal_to_identity(p, &keeper, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf[0..]);
        if (open_as_identity(p, &stranger, frame_buf[0..f1], out_buf[0..])) |_| {
            return error.StrangerOpened;
        } else |_| {}

        var frame_buf2: [max_frame_bytes(p)]u8 = undefined;
        const f2 = try seal_to_identity(p, &keeper, &esk, &m, plain_buf[0..], frame_buf2[0..]);
        if (f1 != f2 or !bytes_eq(frame_buf[0..f1], frame_buf2[0..f2])) return error.DeterminismWrong;
    }
}

fn run_selftest() !u8 {
    try check_param(pq.pq_small);
    try check_param(pq.pq_default);
    try check_param(pq.pq_large);

    print("GREEN crypto-hybrid-sealed: the HYBRID sealed-to-identity door authored in pure Rye — a message sealed TO a hybrid identity so its " ++
        "confidentiality survives a break in EITHER family: the classical X25519 agreement Comlink uses today, AND the post-quantum ML-KEM agreement a " ++
        "lattice identity carries. The confidentiality sibling of crypto/hybrid_identity.rye: where that makes a signature genuine only when BOTH the " ++
        "Ed25519 and ML-DSA halves verify (a forger must break both families), this seals a message readable only when BOTH the classical and the " ++
        "post-quantum agreements open (an eavesdropper must break both families). Authors NO new cryptography and adds NO new primitive — confidentiality " ++
        "is an ONION of two GREEN doors: the inner layer is crypto/kumara_sealed (the classical X25519 box), the outer layer is crypto/mlkem_sealed (the " ++
        "post-quantum ML-KEM box); seal wraps the plaintext in the classical box then that whole frame in the post-quantum box, and open peels the " ++
        "post-quantum layer then the classical one. Proven GREEN on metal at every tier (small ML-KEM-512, default ML-KEM-768, large ML-KEM-1024) with " ++
        "no fabricated constant: (1) a confidential ROUND-TRIP across six plaintext lengths (empty → 4 KiB) recovers the message byte-for-byte through " ++
        "both layers; (2) CONFIDENTIALITY — the sealed frame body is not the plaintext; (3) BREAK-ONE-IS-NOT-ENOUGH — a holder of only the classical seed " ++
        "fails at the outer post-quantum layer (OuterOpenFailed), and a holder of only the ML-KEM secret peels the outer layer yet fails the inner " ++
        "classical one (InnerOpenFailed), so an eavesdropper who breaks one family alone reads nothing, while the true keeper holding both halves opens " ++
        "it; (4) TAMPER — a flipped frame byte refuses; (5) a stranger identity is refused and sealing is deterministic given fixed ephemerals. The " ++
        "module's own functions call no std.crypto — each layer is proven one rung down. Purely local — TEST hybrid identities from published vectors, no " ++
        "real key held, no network, no funds, no real device; both the X25519 ephemeral and the ML-KEM message must be fresh randomness in real use, " ++
        "constant-time timing-safety the named horizon, and sealing to or opening as the maintainer's own hybrid identity the custody gate. With this " ++
        "rung a keeper's message stands on both worlds at once — the eavesdropper held off by two families, and the phrase remembered still only one.\n", .{});
    return 0;
}

pub fn main(init_args: std.process.Init) !u8 {
    _ = init_args;
    return run_selftest();
}
